# homma

homma orchestrates a multi-repository workspace: it knows which repositories the
workspace contains, who works in it, where each participant's work happens, and
how they correspond.

It is deliberately small. Designing and building inside a repository belongs to
that repository's own tooling; homma's job is what spans repositories and what is
true of the workspace rather than of any one member.

## What it must be

**General.** homma runs against a workspace it has never seen. **No key is
required**: the content repository defaults to the configuration's own directory,
and every path has a default homma will create plus an override for a workspace
whose convention differs. So a fresh directory with a registry in it is already a
workspace. Nothing about the workspace homma was born in is compiled in, which is
why layout, roles and record kinds are data rather than code.

Membership follows from that. A directory under the workspace root that is its
own repository is a member, and nothing lists it. Which forge it lives on and
who owns it come from its `origin` remote, which is the thing that decides
where a push lands and is therefore the only honest source for either. A
workspace that renames a repository, clones a new one, or drops one is correct
the moment the tree is, with nothing left saying otherwise.

**Minimal, and delegating.** Where a member repository has its own tooling, homma
drives it rather than reimplementing it. Releasing is the case where the tooling
is the workspace's own: a release is a merge of `dev` onto `main` tagged,
published and announced as one act, gated by a run on the developer's machine
that the forge is told about, and homma drives that because it is the one thing
every workspace has at hand.

## The four primitives

Everything else is a view over these, a kind declared in configuration, or prose
in a generated file.

**The record store.** One engine for everything homma persists. Kinds declare
what attributes a record has and whether it may be edited, so correspondence and
structural work share a write path and differ by a flag.

**The daemon.** Sole writer of the store, sole waker of participants, sole arbiter
of who may act on whose behalf. Three verbs: append a record, deliver what is
waiting, grant a dispatch.

**The registry.** Identity as configuration rather than as a process, so a
participant outlives whatever was running it.

**The generation pass.** Configuration and templates into definitions, settings,
hooks and links; the sole writer of the agent configuration in every workspace it
manages, **the workspace's own rules and skills included**.

## The layers

```
L0 contracts     types and traits, no I/O
L1 mechanism     git, forge, filesystem. things with side effects
L2 store         records and their persistence
L3 domain        registry, correspondence, work
L4 service       the daemon and its surfaces
L5 surface       what a human or an agent drives
```

Crates arrive when something needs them. An empty crate holding a name is a
directory pretending to be a decision.

## What exists

`homma-api` carries the vocabulary. `homma-store` persists records.
`homma-org` holds the registry, the workspace lifecycle and generation.
`homma-engine` is the command surface, and `homma` is the launcher that finds
the workspace, reads the version of the engine it pins, and runs that one.
`homma-core` still holds forge clients and git operations in a pre-adoption
split that a later round dissolves, and it is where the release lives: the gate
that measures a commit, the run record and commit status it leaves, and the
merge, tag, changelog, GitHub release and registry publish that carry `dev`
onto `main`. `homma release` in `homma-engine` is its surface, and
`CORE_RELEASE.md` is the whole of it.

## The rule corpus

A workspace's rules are injected into every session it runs, sub-agents
included, so their size is paid before any work starts. They are authored as
templates and generated, like everything else the generation pass owns.

**One file per rule, and the card is its opening.** The meta as frontmatter, then
the card, then a marker, then the elaboration; the always-loaded card is that
prefix, written by the pass and never by hand. **The card is a prefix of the full
rule rather than a second document**, so nothing is written twice and the two
cannot drift.

Two files were tried first. Frontmatter on one of a pair governs the other
silently, so opening the card template showed none of the meta shaping it, and an
elaboration could lose the card beside it and be authored, findable and absent
from every session at once. **That error class is gone rather than guarded.**

**The meta is what makes a rule findable without its name.** Topics stated in the
words a reader arrives with, and the moment the rule fires. A session about to
write a README asks the subject and gets the governing set; asking by filename
only works for somebody who already knows the answer.

A card whose rule is gone is reported and not deleted. The destination is the
always-loaded set, so such a card is not a stale file sitting in a directory,
it is a document injected into every session for as long as it is there, and
saying nothing about it is the one thing the pass may not do. Deleting it is
the other thing: on disk a card the pass wrote last time and a document
somebody put there on purpose are the same file, and this is the case the skill
corpus below already decides for a directory nothing claims.

An element of an inline list may carry a comma inside quotes, and the split
respects them. A brace group is the ordinary way to write a path pattern, so a
list that split on every comma could not express one at all.

## The skill corpus

The same split, one tier over. A skill is authored as a directory rather than a
file, and what it holds beside its manifest is reference prose, scripts and
sometimes a command-line tool of its own.

**A template is rendered and everything else is copied with its mode.** A shell
script is not prose, and templating one would mean it could not carry a brace
without escaping it.

**The bound is on the description, not on the body.** A body is fetched when a
session reaches for the skill and costs nothing until then; every description is
carried on every session, because the listing is how a session tells which skill
to reach for. An over-budget description is dropped rather than refused, which
leaves the skill named and silent about when it applies.

**Which skills bear on a rule is derived rather than declared**, from citations
in both directions, because a hand-kept list goes wrong the first time a skill is
renamed and nothing reports it.

## The command surface

```
homma org      who exists
homma work     workspaces and what is in them
homma rules    the corpus: what governs a subject, and generating the cards
homma skills   what is authored, and generating the tree a session finds
homma sauna    correspondence and work
homma serve    the daemon and its surfaces
homma release  the gate, the run record, and dev onto main as a release
homma capture  what the person said to a session, copied into the capture store
```

`homma capture --title <title>` reads an agent session's transcript and writes
what the person typed into it, and every question put to them with the options
and the answer, as one capture file in the workspace's capture store. The store
is `captures` under `[paths]` in the manifest, `.data/op-responses/` in the
content repository when the key is absent, and `--into` names another directory
for one run. The transcript is the one `--session` names, by id or by path; else
the session the command runs inside, which the harness names to every shell it
starts as `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`; else the newest session the harness keeps for
this workspace. The harness keeps them under `~/.claude/projects/`, in a
directory named for the real path the session started in, symlinks resolved,
with every character outside `[A-Za-z0-9]` turned into `-`, counted as the
harness counts them, in UTF-16 units, so a character outside the basic plane
is two. A session id is
looked for in the workspace's directory first and then in every other one there,
since a session started inside a member repository keeps its transcript under
that repository's name; an id found nowhere is refused rather than replaced by
another session. A last line with no newline is still being written and is not
read.

The words are copied out of the transcript's structured fields and never
rewritten, so every quoted span equals a string the transcript holds. A line is
the person's when the harness marks its origin as human, since hook output,
compaction summaries, task notifications and prompts the harness sends itself
are all typed lines too and none carries it, unless a tool typed it. A tool that
types into the session's terminal is marked human as well, so such a tool writes
down what it typed beside the transcript, as `<session>.typed-by-tools.ndjson`,
one JSON object per line typed with `at`, the instant before it typed, and
`text`, exactly what it typed. A human line whose words are a recorded `text`,
bare or inside one `<pasted_content id="…">` wrapper the harness puts round a
paste, and which is stamped at or after that record's `at` and no more than
sixty seconds after it, is the tool's; each record takes at most one line, the
first that fits, so the person typing the same words later is still the person.
A tool writes its record before typing and nothing confirms the typing landed,
so the bound is what keeps a failed paste's record from taking the person's own
words later; a tool's line landing past it is quoted as the person's, which is
visible in the file. A
message typed while a turn runs is read where it landed: as a typed line once the
turn ended, or as the queued-command attachment the running turn took it in. The
harness can write one message more than once, as an attachment and a typed line
or as two attachments, and ties the copies by `source_uuid`: every human line
whose `uuid` or `source_uuid` another has named is the same message, read where
the first of them was written. Words are a string or a list of content blocks,
the second when an image was pasted beside them, and blocks are read as their
text blocks in order joined by a blank line; an image alone is nothing said. An
answered question is read whole off its tool result, which carries the
questions, their options, the answers and the notes. A tool result is an ask
round when the `tool_use_id` on its content block names a call the agent made to
`AskUserQuestion`, found on an earlier assistant line with that id and that
`name`, and only then; any other tool's result is not read, whatever keys it
has. A round in which no question has an answer or a note, the harness's own
timeout among them, said nothing and is not read either.

Only the person's words are blockquotes in the file. The voice tool reads every
blockquote in a capture as one of their remarks, so the agent's text around them,
the question it asked and the turn before an unprompted message, goes in fenced
blocks. That text is copied as it stands, which is longer than a summary and
exact. The agent's fields that sit on one line of the file, the title,
a question's header and an option's label, are written with every line break in
them spelled `\n` and every carriage return `\r`, so none of them can open a
line at the margin.

A capture is not a record homma keeps for itself. It is a file written for
another reader, the voice tool, in that reader's format, which makes it prose in
a generated file rather than something the record store holds; the watermark
below is read back out of those files, so it exists once. Its front matter
carries `tags: [chat]`, the store's mark for words said in a session.

A capture names the session it came from and the `uuid` of the last complete
line the run read in its front matter, as `source` and `through`, and a run takes
only the lines after the latest `through` the store records for it. Latest means
latest in the transcript, and the position in the file is the order everything
follows, since the harness stamps a queued message with the time it was queued
and writes it after lines stamped later. So a second run with nothing new writes
nothing and says so, and there is no state file beside the store to go stale. A
`through` the transcript does not hold is refused rather than guessed around. A
session no capture has come from yet has no watermark, and `--since` takes an
RFC 3339 instant as a floor for that first run, keeping only events stamped after
it; given with a watermark it applies as well, which loses the queued messages
stamped before lines written ahead of them, so it is for the first run only.
With neither the run takes the whole session. On a question that takes one answer,
an answer equal to a label is that choice and anything else is what the person
typed, whole. On one that takes several, an answer is read as the chosen labels
first, joined by `, ` with a label that holds `, ` in double quotes, the longest
label tried first, and whatever follows them is what the person typed. The labels
are the agent's and are named in plain text; the harness's `(notes only)` is
dropped; the typed part and the notes are the person's and are quoted. The file
is created new and never replaces one. One run is one capture, in the order things
happened, and its `kind` says whether it holds unprompted words, answered
questions, or both. The title is given rather than made up, because it decides
whether the voice tool reads the capture at all; `bears_on` is what `--bears-on`
names plus every path the words mention that exists under the workspace root,
and a `--bears-on` holding a line break is refused, since it would write a key of
its own into the front matter; `when` is the first event's local time, and the
file is named for it and the title, lowercased, with `ä` and `å` folded to `a`
and `ö` to `o` and anything else outside `[a-z0-9]` a dash. The store, whether
configured or named by `--into`, is checked against the places homma never
writes, the harness's `~/.claude`, other participants' workspaces and whatever
`deny` names, before anything is written, and that list needs the home directory,
so a run with no `HOME` is refused there even for a session named by path. The
command writes the file and stops. Committing it is the caller's, who is on a
branch in the content repository already.

Two commands are the launcher's rather than the engine's, because each runs
where there may be no engine to run. `homma config`, described with the settings
below, is answered by renki for any tool that declares settings, since the
engine is the thing being configured and may not build. `homma workspace` reports the workspace the cwd is
in, or makes one where it is not: bare, inside a workspace, it prints the root
and every repository in it with its branch, whether the tree is dirty and how
many commits sit on no remote; bare, outside one, it clones the content
repository into the cwd, which has to be empty; `spawn <slug>` does the same
under the person's workspaces directory, with the repositories named beside
it; `reap` removes one, refusing while anything in it is dirty or unpushed,
and refusing outright a target that is not a workspace, one the settings
deny, one the cwd is inside, or one holding a worktree, since a seat is
somebody's; `list` names what is there. It is the session's throwaway clone and knows
nothing about identities. The org design's workspace lifecycle, which stands
a participant's workspace up from a registry entry inside a root, is a
different thing and stays in the engine.

What that command needs to know is the person's, not the workspace's, so it
is the first thing homma reads out of a user settings file. The launcher's
descriptor declares four keys through renki's configuration surface, all user
scope: `disallowed_roots`, the directories a workspace may never be made in,
defaulting to the home directory itself with a `/*` suffix denying a whole
subtree; `workspaces_root`, where `spawn <slug>` puts one; `spawn.content_repo`,
the git url a fresh workspace is a clone of, empty by default and refusing to
spawn until set, since a default of the author's own repository is the
author's arrangement compiled in; and `spawn.repos`, what every fresh
workspace clones beside it. `homma config` reads and writes the file, and the
manifest's `deny` list is a different question, where homma may write inside
a workspace rather than where one may be made.

A call with a terminal attached should print for a human, and a call without one
should print its result and nothing else, because something is parsing it. That
split is decided and **not yet built**: nothing in the tree senses a terminal, and
`homma org` prints the same way either way.

Two commands read and write the shared tool configs described in the org
design. `homma repo config check` compares a repository against them and reports;
it exits non-zero when something is missing that the repository is meant to have.
`homma repo config init` places what is missing. They are two commands rather
than one because the check runs on a path that must not write, and placing a
config is a deliberate act.

Which non-zero it is carries the rest of the answer, and the split belongs to
the binary rather than to these two commands. Zero is a command that ran with
nothing to say, `1` is one that ran and reported, and `2` is one that could not
run at all, which is what any error reaching the entry point returns. A caller
has no other way to ask, and the commonest thing behind a `2` here is the
workspace manifest failing to parse, which is nobody's repository's fault.

That path is the gate. A workspace-level hook already stands in front of
`git commit` and `git push` from inside any member repository, works out which
repository the call came from, and refuses when that repository's mockspace
bootstrap is incomplete. It gains the config check, and refuses the same way,
naming the command that fixes it where there is one. Where the check could not
run it says that instead, and names the workspace manifest, because sending
somebody to a command that fails the same way is worse than saying nothing.

What it means by incomplete is a repository showing some of mockspace's
surfaces and not others, since none of them is a repository that never opted
in and all of them is one with nothing to say. `core.hooksPath` being set was
one of those surfaces on the reading that mockspace is what sets it, and that
reading is no longer safe: git-shook points the same setting at a dispatcher
directory of its own, in repositories that may never have heard of mockspace,
and the gate then reads one surface of three and refuses every commit there
with an instruction that cannot help. What the gate is asking is whether
mockspace's hooks fire, so a path that is git-shook's answers no and counts as
no, and a path that is anything else counts as before.

The per-repository half is one entrypoint per git event in the repository's
own hooks directory, written by homma and reading its entries off `[hooks]` in
the workspace manifest, the release deep dive has the shape. mockspace owns
`core.hooksPath` in every repository it manages and homma never contends for
it: mockspace's gate runs the repository's own hook after its own checks, so
git's own directory is the one place both reach, and homma writes there and
nowhere else. The workspace hook stays one file in one place beside that,
which matters because the per-repository wiring is exactly the thing that can
be quietly absent.

The gate asks homma rather than deciding for itself, so the rule that says what
a repository owes exists once. It refuses when it cannot ask at all, for the
same reason it refuses when it cannot read its input: a check that could not run
must never be indistinguishable from one that ran and found nothing.

## The agent hooks

An agent session opened at the workspace root loads the workspace's own
`.claude/settings.json` and nothing from the repositories under it, so every
hook a repository's mockspace generates reaches such a session only if homma
carries it up. `homma agent regen` does that, and it owes the same thing to
those hooks that git-shook owes to git's: every one of them keeps running, at
the event and for the tools it was written for, and nothing homma did not write
is replaced or removed.

The host runs every hook registered for an event, in parallel, and runs a
handler named twice only once. So the dispatcher git needs, one program at an
event fanning out to many, is not needed here, and one registration per hook is
what homma writes. A dispatcher would put a homma process in front of every
tool call and serialise hooks the host already runs side by side, to buy an
order nothing here depends on.

What is homma's is decided by what homma wrote. Every file it writes under
`.claude/hooks/` carries a line saying it was written by `homma agent regen`,
and a registration is homma's only when the file it names carries that line.
Anything else is kept exactly as it is, and a file homma would have to overwrite
to write its own is refused by path, with the regen reporting it and exiting
`1`. A name alone decides nothing, since a hand-written hook can carry any name.

Each repository hook is registered under every event and matcher its
repository's `settings.json` gives it, and under the matchers its own
`@matchers:` line names where the repository gives none. A hook that has
neither is not written as a wrapper nobody calls: it is reported by path, and
the regen exits `1`. Any executable file under the repository's `.claude/hooks/`
is a hook, whatever language it is in, and a file there that is not executable
and that no registration runs through a program is reported the same way, since
the host could not run it either.

A workspace declares hooks of its own in the manifest, under `[agent.hooks]`,
one array per event named as the host names it:

```toml
[[agent.hooks.PreToolUse]]
matcher = "Bash"
run     = "scripts/keep-the-build-off-this-machine"
repos   = ["engine"]
```

`run` is a path under the workspace, and `repos` narrows the hook to calls
landing in those repositories, the way every aggregated hook is narrowed to its
own. Without it the hook sees every call. homma writes a wrapper for each row
beside the aggregated ones, carrying the same line, so the rows are kept and
removed by the same rule, and a guard a workspace wants in every session is a
line in the manifest rather than a hand edit to a generated file.

A call is narrowed by where it lands, and a hook sees it when any place it
lands is under the hook's repository. For a tool writing a file that is the
file's path, and a path that climbs with `..` lands everywhere. For a shell call it is the directory the host says the session is
in, and every path the command names as well, read relative to that directory,
since `cargo test --manifest-path engine/Cargo.toml` typed at the workspace
root lands in `engine` whatever directory it was typed in. Where the host says
nothing about a directory, the hook process's own working directory stands in,
and that is the workspace root whatever the session did.

A wrapper is not a shell, so it reads a command by splitting it on everything
that cannot be part of a path, which takes quotes, separators, redirections and
the `=` in `--flag=path` apart, and reading each piece as a path. It also looks
for a repository's absolute root anywhere in the command, and a piece that
climbs with `..` counts as landing everywhere, since a guard skipped costs what
the guard is for. A guard run once too often costs a process where the guard
only observes. Where it denies, it costs a false refusal: a bare word equal to a
repository's path, as in `echo engine`, lands there, and a guard that denies on
anything it is handed denies a call that touched nothing of its repository.
What the wrapper cannot see is a relative path with a space in it, a path
spelled through a variable, and whatever a script the command runs goes on to
do. A guard that needs those reads the command itself.

A hook the wrapper runs is run in the directory the session is in, and handed
the payload exactly as the host sent it. Running it from its repository's root
instead would satisfy a hook that asks its own working directory where it is,
and would break every hook that reads a relative path out of the payload, since
that path is relative to the session's directory. So the wrapper reaching a hook
decides only that the hook runs, and what it then allows is the hook's own
reading of the payload. A hook that scopes itself by its working directory
declines a call typed elsewhere that names its repository by path, however the
wrapper routed it, and scoping by the payload's directory and the command's
paths is what makes it see one.

A registration names its hook by the first word of its command that reaches
into `.claude/hooks/`. Whatever precedes that word is the program the host runs
it with, as in `bash .claude/hooks/check.sh`, and whatever follows is its
arguments, both of which the wrapper carries. A hook run through a program does
not have to be executable. A hook that two registrations run through two
different programs is reported by path and not carried, since one wrapper can
only run it one way. So is a hook whose program is a line of shell rather than a
program, an assignment in front of it or a separator before it, since the
wrapper runs a program with arguments and a shell line would run as something
else: `cd dir && .claude/hooks/x.sh` would run a `cd` binary and report its
success as the hook's.

A file under a repository's `.claude/hooks/` whose name starts with a dot is an
editor's or a filesystem's, and is neither carried nor reported. A registration
in the workspace naming a file homma writes, where that file is no longer
there, is removed on the next regen, since nothing it could run exists.

## What a status answers

`homma status` answers what state the workspace is in. That question is best
served by what is wrong rather than by an inventory of what is fine, so it covers
every population homma can report and shows, by default, only what is not in the
state it should be in. A repository with nothing to say about it takes one line.
`--full` prints every population whole, for when that is what somebody wants.

The narrower verbs are unchanged and stay the place to see one population
entirely, healthy entries included. With the plain status there is no need to
know which of them to reach for.

Covered: the workspace and its forges, what this clone of it is for, the agent
surfaces including whether the repository's git hooks are wired at all, the
shared tool configs, and the working tree. The doc surfaces are deliberately not, being a survey of what a repository
has rather than a report of anything being wrong, and nothing offline is left
out for cost. Nothing here reaches the network, which is what makes it the
command to run first.

The machine-readable document grows fields rather than changing shape, so what
reads it today keeps working and simply ignores what it does not know. It is
never summarised, whatever the human output is doing: a document that quietly
omitted the healthy members would be misdescribing the population to the one
reader with no way to notice.

## What a clone is for

The manifest is committed and every clone of the content repository shares it,
so it says only what is true of the workspace everywhere. The same content
repository is cloned once per body of work, and what one clone is for is a fact
about that clone alone. It lives in `homma.local.toml`, beside the manifest and
never committed.

Two tables. `[instance]` is homma's and typed: `work` names the body of work and
is the one key required, `title` is what to call it, `repos` narrows which
members the work spans and means all of them when empty, and `state` and `goal`
are paths from the workspace root to the document carrying the work's current
state and the one carrying what it is trying to reach. The file points at those
documents and decides nothing about them; where a workspace keeps its state and
which goal is current are its own conventions. `[tools.<name>]` belongs to the
workspace's tools and is carried untyped, for the reason `[[status.inject]]`
runs a tool without knowing it: homma holds a tool's settings without growing
an opinion about them.

The manifest's loader does not read it. Only `status` and `local` use the file,
and they read it themselves, anchored beside the manifest, because every other
command, the commit and push gates among them, has nothing to learn from it and
no reason to refuse over it. Absent is the ordinary case and nothing notices.
Present and not parsing is never silently ignored either: `status` gives it a
line of its own naming the file and the error, and `local show` fails on it.

`homma local init --work <name>` writes the file with every key in it, refuses
to overwrite one that exists, and adds `/homma.local.toml` and
`/homma.local.toml.*` to the workspace's `.gitignore`, each where no line names
it yet: a file described as never committed that is one `git add -A` from being
committed is a file that will be, and the second line covers the lock and the
temporary file `set` leaves behind when it dies halfway.
`homma local show` prints it, the document whole with `--output json`, so a
script reads it through homma's parser rather than a second one; no file is
exit `1`, a command that ran and reported. `homma local set <table.key> <value>`
writes one string under `instance` or under `tools.<name>` and nowhere else,
keeps every comment around it, and refuses a result that would not parse. It
exists so a hook can record what it learned without anybody editing the file,
and a hook runs beside whatever else reads the file, so the read, edit and write
happen under a lock file beside it, created exclusively and retried for a
bounded time before `set` refuses, and the result goes to a temporary file in
the same directory renamed over the original. A reader sees the old file or the
new one and never a half, and two writers at once both land.

`homma status` carries the instance, one line in the human output and the
table whole in the document, `null` where there is no file. Where the file does
not parse the instance is `null` and `instance_error` carries the message.
